### Test Organization

- Use pytest fixtures for common setup (empty files, test files, mocked configurations)
- Create test cases at multiple levels:
  - Unit tests for individual functions
  - Integration tests for component interactions
  - CLI tests for command-line interfaces
  - End-to-end tests for full workflows

### Test Case Design

- Test both success and failure scenarios for every feature
- Include edge cases (empty files, maximum file sizes, etc.)
- Test all CLI options and combinations
- Cover error handling paths thoroughly
- Validate error messages and exit codes
- Test progress reporting and callback mechanisms
- Verify proper torrent metadata and structure
- Create tests that span file types (single files vs. directories)

### CLI Testing Best Practices

- Use click.testing.CliRunner for CLI tests
- Test dry-run modes to verify output without file creation
- Validate output messages match expected formats
- For file operations that might already exist, include --force flag tests
- Test verbose output modes (-v flag)
- Test skip_empty_files functionality in both success and failure modes
- Verify tracker URL validation in both success and failure modes

### Test Implementation Guidelines

- Avoid over-mocking core functionality
- Use real torrent creation for integration tests
- Mock only external dependencies and slow operations
- Use isolated filesystem for file operation tests
- Clean up test artifacts after test completion
- Include appropriate assertions for all test outcomes
- Structure tests to be deterministic and repeatable
- Implement tests following the Arrange-Act-Assert pattern

### Performance and Security Testing

- Include tests for minimum/maximum piece size validation
- Test handling of malicious or invalid inputs
- Verify that file overwrite protection works correctly
- Test handling of concurrent operations when applicable
- Validate behavior with extremely large file sizes
- Test cleanup operations and manifest maintenance
